[38;5;231m⏺[39m Security/compliance is the constraint that matters most, and it pushes hard against the original shape of the request.
  A compliance audit trail can't be produced by a browser: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m runs on the user's machine, so anything it reports
  about who logged in is attacker-controlled — it can be forged, suppressed, or replayed. For "who accessed what, when"
  to hold up, the record has to be written by whatever actually authenticates the credentials.

  [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m points at [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m and [38;5;153mlogin[39m is explicitly a stub ("would POST to API_ENDPOINT in real
  app"). So the real authentication lives somewhere outside this repo, and that's where the audit record belongs. Which
  makes this the next thing I need to know:
